module gunbc.live_deploy.slice_bounds

import std.measure { ByteSize, byte_size }

// ── THE CGROUP BOUNDS THIS DEPLOYMENT'S SERVED PROCESSES RUN UNDER ──────────────────────────
// THEY LIVE IN A LEAF MODULE BECAUSE TWO LAYERS NEED THEM AND ONE OF THEM IS UPSTREAM OF THE
// EMITTER. gunbc.live_deploy.emit renders them into slice units; gunbc.live_deploy.served_slice_charge
// charges them against the host budget, and the budget is reached from the emitter's own import
// closure (emit -> ci_deploy_access -> ... -> ci_runner_placement -> fleet_host_budget). Declaring
// the numbers in the emitter therefore closed an import cycle, which CI's floor caught as
// `circular dependency detected` where the parse sweep could not see it -- acyclicity is the import
// graph's only structural law (DESIGN section 3) and a policy row is not an emission fact.
//
// So the bounds sit where both consumers can reach them and neither can be reached FROM: this
// module imports one std measure module and nothing else.

// THE DEPLOYMENT'S BOUND IS ITS OWN, AND THE SHARED-IDENTITY ARGUMENT THAT MADE IT THE RUNNER
// SLOT'S IS FALSIFIED RATHER THAN OVERRULED (2026-09-12).
//
// WHAT THIS ROW USED TO SAY, and it was true when written: "the bound is the runner slot's, BY
// IDENTITY OF WORKLOAD rather than by coincidence of number -- the deployment's oneshots run
// `gunbc run` over the same corpus a CI job resolves, and the server is the same binary, so
// gunbc_runner_slot_desired is the fleet's one authored allowance for that process and a second
// literal here would be the section-3 fork."
//
// WHAT FALSIFIED IT: the runner row stopped being an allowance for "a gunbc process". The
// 2026-09-12 demand ruling sizes it from the REQUIRED FLOOR'S measured peak -- one prepared subject
// over the whole corpus, 22.74 GiB uncensored -- which is work this deployment never performs. The
// same 2026-09-04 measurement the old note cited for scale is the refutation at this size: serve
// peaked at 3.46 GB and the belt at 0.97 GB. Sharing one number with the floor was a coincidence of
// magnitude that read as an identity of workload, and the moment the floor's number moved, the
// coincidence ended while the sentence asserting the identity did not. That is the pattern this
// corpus keeps finding -- a name that states a coincidence as a law.
//
// THE VALUES DO NOT CHANGE TODAY, deliberately. They are the bytes this deployment already runs
// with, restated as its own fact with its own justification rather than re-derived to something
// tighter in the same change that decouples them. Whether 16 GiB is the right bound for a 4.43 GB
// workload is a separate question with its own evidence; this change only stops the answer being
// decided by the floor's memory demand.
//
// IT ALSO CLOSES A CAP-WRITER BYPASS. `live_deploy` writes this slice with no host admission
// consulted, so while the number was the runner row's, any move to that row propagated to a host
// whose runner allocation may have been REFUSED. Decoupling removes the propagation rather than
// gating it: there is no longer a runner-derived number here to leak.
// THE ENVELOPE IS SIZED FROM THE UNIT'S OWN MEASURED PEAK, not from the 3.46 GB the 2026-09-12
// note cited. With swap forbidden below, the bound stopped being decorative: at the 16 GiB bound the
// serve process -- which systemd's unit accounting reported at "8.1G memory peak, 9.6G memory swap
// peak" for its previous incarnation -- was OOM-killed inside the slice on 2026-09-18 23:07 UTC, and
// the deployed-tree convergence oneshot ("5.2G memory peak") beside its restart at 23:44. Swap had
// been absorbing the ~2 GB the serve workload exceeds the old bound by, silently. The instrument is
// the unit's journal "Consumed ... memory peak, ... memory swap peak" line (systemd unit accounting)
// for gunbc-roadmap.service and gunbc-tree-sync.service; re-derive there. 26 GiB max / 24 GiB high
// (operator ruling 2026-09-18, the same envelope the runner slots moved to that day) covers serve
// peak + convergence peak concurrently (~23 GB); why the serve footprint grew from 3.46 GB to ~18 GB
// is a demand question (DESIGN section 2) this row does not answer and does not hide. That this
// number EQUALS the runner slot's is a coincidence of two rulings on one day, not a derivation: the
// row is authored here, and the witness pins that by reading this declaration, not by inequality.
data live_deploy_slice_memory_max: ByteSize = byte_size(27917287424)

data live_deploy_slice_memory_high: ByteSize = byte_size(25769803776)

// THE SLICE FORBIDS SWAP, because the deployment is a latency-bound operator-facing service and a
// swapped page is a stall on the request path, not a memory saving. The host it shares (srv1) also
// runs the required floor's runners, whose working sets outrank an idle interpreter heap under
// global reclaim; the observed state before this row was the serve process at VmSwap 9.7 GB against
// VmRSS 0.4 GB, and every request after an idle gap paying the swap-in -- the 5-10 s /healthz and
// /approvals stalls that refused a live deploy and two approval-gated boot transactions (GH runs
// 35377916920 and 35380154698, both fail-closed on a poll that returned no bytes in 5 s). The
// instrument is /proc/<serve pid>/status VmSwap beside the unit's journal gap; re-derive there, not
// from this sentence. Zero rather than a bound: any admitted swap is admitted latency. Growth stays
// bounded by MemoryHigh and MemoryMax above -- a hard kernel boundary (throttling, allocation
// failure, or the cgroup's own OOM kill), not an application refusal value -- so the deployment
// fails or restarts inside its declared envelope rather than becoming a silently swapped control
// plane. Lowering swap.max below existing usage does NOT drain what is already paged out: the live
// closure is a new service incarnation whose MainPID reads VmSwap 0 kB and answers /healthz and
// one status GET inside the client bound.
data live_deploy_slice_memory_swap_max: ByteSize = byte_size(0)

// ── THE APPROVAL BROKER'S OWN BOUND ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE DERIVATION THIS ROW ORIGINALLY CARRIED IS FALSIFIED, AND THE NUMBER SURVIVES ON A WEAKER
// FOOTING RATHER THAN THE OLD ONE RESTATED. It read: the pre-split roadmap process served these
// four routes, so its measured 3.46 GB peak bounds a strict subset of its closure, and 6 GiB is
// comfortably above that. The containment half is still true -- the broker's closure IS a subset of
// the pre-split roadmap's. The MEASUREMENT half is not: the row above now records the same serve
// process OOM-killed at a 16 GiB bound on 2026-09-18 with an 8.1 GB resident peak and 9.6 GB of
// swap, and states outright that the growth from 3.46 GB to roughly 18 GB is an unanswered demand
// question. A ceiling proved by containment against that superset is about 26 GiB, which bounds
// nothing useful.
//
// SO THIS IS A DECLARED POLICY BUDGET WITH AN UNMEASURED SUBJECT, said plainly because DESIGN 4d
// forbids carrying an inference in the vocabulary of a deduction. What supports 6 GiB is a
// qualitative fact -- the broker compiles and serves four routes that consult no roadmap, no plan,
// no site bundle and no attempt evidence, which is the work the roadmap's footprint is made of --
// and that is a reason to expect a much smaller process, not a measurement of one.
//
// WHAT THE RISK ACTUALLY IS, IF THE NUMBER IS WRONG. With MemorySwapMax=0 below, a broker that
// exceeds this bound is killed at the boundary and restarted by systemd: a loud, attributable
// failure on its own lifecycle. That is the failure mode the split exists to produce, and it is
// strictly better than the one this whole change is a response to -- a process silently swapped
// until an approval poll times out. A wrong bound here costs a restart; a shared heap cost boot 15.
//
// THE MEASUREMENT IS STAGE 3 AND THE PLAN REFUSES WITHOUT IT. gunbc.auth.approval_broker_cutover
// will not admit repointing the front door while BrokerDemandReading is DemandUnmeasured, so the
// six readings -- cold bind and load peak, idle resident, submission-plus-notification peak,
// confirm-page peak, redemption peak, status-poll peak -- are taken against the dark broker before
// anything routes to it, and these two rows are tightened from them. The instrument is the unit's
// journal "Consumed ... memory peak" line for gunbc-approval-broker.service, the same one the row
// above names; re-derive there rather than from this sentence.
data approval_broker_slice_memory_max: ByteSize = byte_size(6442450944)

data approval_broker_slice_memory_high: ByteSize = byte_size(5368709120)
